🔥 1. Problem (Before Java 8)
👉 To implement an interface, we had to:
Create separate class
Write extra code (boilerplate)

❌ Old Way (Separate Class):
interface Test{
void m1();
}

class MyClass implements Test{
public void m1(){
sout("hello");
}
}

public class main{
psvm{
Test t=new Myclass();
t.m1();
}
}
🚫 Problem:
Too much code
Separate class needed
Not clean

🔥 2. Anonymous Inner Class (Improvement)
👉 No need to create separate class
interface Test{
void m1();
}

public class Main{
psvm{
Test t =new Test(){
public void m1(){
sout("hello..");
}
};
t.m1();
}
}

👍 Advantage:
No separate class
👎 Still Problem:
Syntax is long and complex

🔥 3. Lambda Expression (Java 8)
👉 Shortest and cleanest way
✅ Lambda Version

interface Test{
void m1();
}
public class Main{
psvm{
Test t=()->sout("hello...");
t.m1();
}
}

🚀 Advantage:
Very short
No boilerplate
Easy to read

