🔥 Final Comparison Table

| Feature                     | Normal Class | Anonymous Class | Lambda  |
| --------------------------- | ------------ | --------------- | ------- |
| Separate class              | ✅ Yes        | ❌ No            | ❌ No    |
| Code length                 | ❌ Long       | ⚠️ Medium       | ✅ Short |
| Readability                 | ❌ Low        | ⚠️ Medium       | ✅ High  |
| Java version                | All          | All             | Java 8+ |
| Functional interface needed | ❌ No         | ❌ No            | ✅ Yes   |

✅ Anonymous Inner Class
Class without name
Used for one-time use
Can implement interface or extend class

✅ Lambda Expression
Introduced in Java 8
Used for functional interfaces
Represents only method implementation

👉 Anonymous Class:
Can have multiple methods
Has its own class
👉 Lambda:
Only for one abstract method
No class creation
Just method logic

"Before Java 8, we used separate classes or anonymous inner classes to
 implement interfaces, which increased boilerplate code. Java 8 introduced
 lambda expressions, which provide a concise way to implement functional
 interfaces by writing only the method logic,
improving readability and reducing code size.

