🔥 Anonymous Inner Class vs Lambda (Interview Points):
____________________________________________________________
Anonymous Class:
-A class without a name
-Can have multiple methods
-Anonymous Class 👉 More boilerplate code ❌
-Functional Interface Requirement
 Anonymous Class 👉 ❌ Not required
-Syntax
 Anonymous Class
 Test t = new Test() {
     public void m1() {
         System.out.println("Hello");
     }
 };
-this Keyword Behavior ⭐ (Very Important)
 Anonymous Class 👉 this refers to anonymous class object
-Performance
 Anonymous Class 👉 Slightly heavier
-Use Case
 Anonymous Class 👉 When multiple methods or complex logic needed



Lambda :
-Just implementation of a method (no class)
-Only works with one abstract method (functional interface)
-Lambda 👉 Very short and clean ✅
-Functional Interface Requirement
 Lambda 👉 ✅ Must be functional interface
 -SYntax:
 Lambda
 Test t = () -> System.out.println("Hello");
 -this Keyword Behavior ⭐ (Very Important)
  Lambda 👉 this refers to enclosing class object
-Performance
   Lambda 👉 More efficient (less overhead)
-Use Case
 Lambda 👉 When single method logic (functional programming)



 "Anonymous inner class is a way to create a class without a name
  and can contain multiple methods, whereas lambda expression is a
  concise way to implement a functional interface with a single abstract method.
   Lambda reduces boilerplate code and improves readability. Also,
    'this' in lambda refers to enclosing class, while in anonymous class
    it refers to the anonymous object."
